{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790439151,"nonce":"s2exfmMPhC9EPyn/+dijUCE8yt8=","title":"Interpreter: a list of tuples with a bool field misaligns its stores","message":"A list whose elements are a tuple shape with a bool field panics in the\nHIR interpreter:\n\n    def f():\n        xs = [(\"a\", True), (\"b\", False)]\n        xs.append((\"c\", True))\n        print(xs, xs[2][1])\n    f()\n\npanics with \"misaligned pointer dereference: address must be a multiple\nof 0x8\" in hir_interp.rs write_typed (the i64 arm), with\nZYNTAX_BASELINE_THRESHOLD=1 as well. The element struct (ptr, bool) is\n16 bytes padded and 9 unpadded; the addresses written (…b2, …21) look\nlike an element stride without the tail padding somewhere between the\nlist storage and the interpreter's struct layout (size_of_hir_ty pads).\n\nThe typed dict and set stores keep bool values, and tuples holding a\nbool, out of their slots (boxed instead) until this is fixed; a dict\nentry (i64, str, bool) hits the same panic.","files":null}]}